[
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("AFG"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Afghanistan",
        leader_name: "Mohammed Zahir Shah",
        leader_title: "King",
        government: "Absolute Monarchy",
        situation: "You have worn the crown since 1933, when your father was assassinated before your eyes, though your uncles have long governed in your name; Prime Minister Shah Mahmud runs the ministries while you preside from the Arg. Afghanistan survived the Great Game by bending to no empire, and you kept it neutral through the world war. Now the Game resumes under new names: the Soviet Union sits on your northern rivers, and the British Raj has been replaced by a new neighbor, Pakistan, that you refuse to fully accept.\n\nA loya jirga has just repudiated the Durand Line after Pakistani aircraft bombed a border village, and Kabul trumpets the cause of Pashtunistan for the tribes beyond the frontier. Your requests to Washington for arms are politely declined; Moscow is more patient, and more dangerous. A cautious liberal parliament tests reform at home while mullahs and tribes watch. Neutrality made your dynasty; the question is whether it can keep it.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The Great Game never ended; play it again.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ALB"),
        display_name: "People's Republic of Albania",
        leader_name: "Enver Hoxha",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister and Party General Secretary",
        government: "Stalinist Dictatorship",
        situation: "You are Enver Hoxha, master of Europe's poorest and most isolated nation. When Stalin expelled Tito from the Cominform in 1948, you seized the moment to break Yugoslavia's suffocating grip on Albania, tearing up the treaties that were quietly annexing your country. Koçi Xoxe, the interior minister who served Belgrade's interests, was hanged in June 1949. Now you answer to Moscow alone — a Soviet client with no land connection to the bloc, wedged between a hostile Yugoslavia and a Greece that still claims your southern districts.\n\nThe West has decided you are the bloc's softest target. Émigré commandos backed by London and Washington are landing on your coasts and dropping into your mountains — and, betrayed from within their own operation, they are walking into your ambushes. The Hague court has just ordered you to pay Britain for the Corfu Channel minings; you refuse. Seal the borders, arm the villages, and turn Albania into a fortress.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Europe's smallest fortress defies every neighbor.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("AND"),
        display_name: "Valleys of Andorra",
        leader_name: "Francesc Cairat",
        leader_title: "First Syndic of the General Council",
        government: "Feudal Co-Principality",
        situation: "High in the Pyrenees, six thousand people live under a constitution sealed in 1278. Your co-princes are an odd couple — the President of the French Republic and the Bishop of Urgell in Franco's Spain — while real business falls to the General Council of the Valleys, where heads of families elect their own, and to First Syndic Francesc Cairat, in office since before the Spanish Civil War ended. There are no political parties, no army, and no votes for women.\n\nThe world wars made Andorra quietly rich: smuggling, then duty-free trade, and the powerful transmitters of Radio Andorra, which infuriate Paris. Neutrality between Franco's Spain and the Fourth Republic is your oldest craft, practiced for seven centuries. But the roads are improving, tourists are discovering tax-free shopping, and money is flowing into a medieval constitution never designed to hold it. Guard the old liberties while the twentieth century climbs the mountain passes toward you.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Defend seven centuries of feudal neutrality between Franco and France.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ARG"),
        display_name: "Argentine Republic",
        leader_name: "Juan Domingo Perón",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Populist Authoritarian Republic",
        situation: "You are Perón, and Argentina is yours. The descamisados who carried you to power in 1946 have been repaid with wages, pensions, and dignity; Evita's foundation feeds the poor and her voice electrifies the masses; the railways have been bought back from the British; and a new constitution written in 1949 permits your re-election. You preach the Third Position — neither Yankee capitalism nor Soviet communism — and dream of an Argentina that leads Latin America as an industrial power answering to no one.\n\nBut the golden years are ending. The postwar boom that filled your coffers is fading, grain harvests disappoint, inflation eats the workers' raises, and the state marketing machine that funded industry by squeezing the farmers is running dry. The oligarchy despises you, the navy has never forgiven you, and the Church watches your cult of personality with narrowing eyes. The 1951 election approaches, and Evita's ambitions — perhaps a place on the ticket itself — could bind the movement to you forever or provoke the men with epaulets.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Perón's New Argentina bows to no empire.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("AUS"),
        display_name: "Commonwealth of Australia",
        leader_name: "Robert Menzies",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "Robert Menzies is back. Two weeks ago his new Liberal Party swept away eight years of Labor rule, campaigning against socialism, bank nationalization and petrol rationing — and on a promise to outlaw the Communist Party of Australia outright. The migration program is transforming the nation: 'populate or perish' brings shiploads of Britons and displaced Europeans, and the vast Snowy Mountains hydroelectric scheme has just broken ground.\n\nFear drives the mandate. China fell to Mao in October; the Malayan Emergency smoulders to the north; communist officials lead your most powerful unions. The Communist Party Dissolution Bill will consume 1950 — and the High Court, and perhaps a referendum, may yet have their say. Abroad you honour Britain but court America, seeking the Pacific security pact London can no longer provide. And if war comes to Asia, your wool will sell at any price — and your soldiers may be called for once again.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Menzies versus the communists — at home and abroad.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("AUT"),
        display_name: "Republic of Austria",
        leader_name: "Leopold Figl",
        leader_title: "Federal Chancellor",
        government: "Occupied Democracy",
        situation: "You govern a free republic inside an occupied country. Four armies — American, British, French, and Soviet — have partitioned Austria into zones for nearly five years, and Vienna itself, marooned deep in the Soviet zone, is carved into four sectors patrolled by jeeps carrying one soldier of each power. Leopold Figl, the peasant-born chancellor who survived Dachau, leads a grand coalition of Catholics and Socialists just re-elected in October 1949, governing with a unity born of shared imprisonment under the Nazis.\n\nAustria's claim to be Hitler's first victim has won it a government but not a treaty: negotiations for the State Treaty that would end the occupation drag on endlessly, while the Soviets bleed the eastern zone through seized 'German assets' — the oil fields, the Danube shipping, whole factories. Marshall aid flows in regardless, rebuilding the western zones. Keep the coalition united, the Communists marginal, and the occupiers talking; one misstep could freeze the Iron Curtain across the Alps forever — or split Austria as Germany was split.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Four armies hold Vienna; talk them all into leaving.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("BEL"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Belgium",
        leader_name: "Gaston Eyskens",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "You govern a kingdom with an empty throne. King Leopold III sits in Swiss exile, unforgiven by half his people for surrendering in 1940 and remaining under German occupation while his government fought on from London. His brother Charles reigns as Regent, but the 'Royal Question' poisons everything: Catholic Flanders demands the King's return, socialist Wallonia swears he will never reign again, and Gaston Eyskens's fragile coalition has agreed to put a monarch's fate to a popular referendum — a constitutional novelty that may settle the question or set the country ablaze.\n\nBeneath the crisis, Belgium is quietly indispensable. Its industry recovered faster than any neighbour's, its franc is strong, Brussels hosts the Western Union, and the Congo yields copper, rubber — and the uranium that fed the atom bombs. A founding NATO member wedged on Europe's invasion highway, you must navigate between Flemish and Walloon, King and street, before the referendum's verdict forces every Belgian to choose a side.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "One referendum may break the kingdom in two.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("BGR"),
        display_name: "People's Republic of Bulgaria",
        leader_name: "Vulko Chervenkov",
        leader_title: "General Secretary, Bulgarian Communist Party",
        government: "Communist One-Party State",
        situation: "You are Vulko Chervenkov, and Bulgaria is falling into your hands. Georgi Dimitrov, your brother-in-law and the party's giant, died in a Moscow sanatorium in July 1949. Prime Minister Kolarov, old and gravely ill, is fading fast, leaving you — General Secretary since July — as the real master of the party. Traicho Kostov, your greatest rival, hanged in December after a show trial in which he stunned the courtroom by recanting his confession; tens of thousands of his alleged sympathizers are being combed out of the party ranks.\n\nBulgaria is Moscow's most loyal satellite, and you intend to be its little Stalin. Collectivization advances faster here than anywhere else in the bloc. But every border is hostile: Tito's heretic Yugoslavia to the west, defeated but bitter Greece to the south, Turkey and its restless Turkish minority question to the southeast. The premiership is within your reach — take it, and remake Bulgaria in your patron's image.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The old men are dying. Bulgaria is yours.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("BOL"),
        display_name: "Republic of Bolivia",
        leader_name: "Mamerto Urriolagoitía",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Oligarchic Republic",
        situation: "You govern a country that is a mountain of tin ruled by three men who own it. The great barons — Patiño, Hochschild, Aramayo — and their political servants, la rosca, have run Bolivia for generations, while Indian miners cough out their lungs at fourteen thousand feet for starvation wages. President Mamerto Urriolagoitía holds the palace, but only just: in 1949 the revolutionary MNR rose in half the cities of the republic, and it took the army weeks of real civil war to put them down. Víctor Paz Estenssoro directs the movement from exile in Buenos Aires, and the mining camps remember Catavi's massacred strikers.\n\nTin prices sag, the treasury leans on a single commodity, and every concession to the miners enrages the barons who fund the state. Elections are due in 1951, and the MNR is banned but far from broken. You are sitting on the most explosive social order in South America — hold it down, reform it, or be buried by it.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Tin barons above, revolution below.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("BRA"),
        display_name: "United States of Brazil",
        leader_name: "Eurico Gaspar Dutra",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Presidential Democracy",
        situation: "You command the colossus of South America — half a continent of coffee, iron, and fifty million people straining toward industry. President Eurico Dutra, the stolid general who succeeded the Vargas dictatorship, has governed under the democratic constitution of 1946: he banned the Communist Party, broke with Moscow, and tied Brazil firmly to Washington, though the promised flood of American development dollars has proven a trickle. São Paulo's factories multiply, but inflation gnaws at wages and the countryside remains a world of oxcarts and oligarchs.\n\nOne shadow dominates the year ahead: Getúlio Vargas. The old dictator, deposed in 1945, sits on his ranch in São Borja giving interviews, courting the workers who remember his labor laws, and preparing his return through the ballot box he once ignored. October's presidential election will decide whether the New State's father comes back as democracy's champion. Manage the succession, the economy, and the generals who loathe Getúlio — Brazil's rise, and its stability, both hang on 1950.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Vargas is coming back. Decide what that means.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("BTN"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Bhutan",
        leader_name: "Jigme Wangchuck",
        leader_title: "Druk Gyalpo",
        government: "Absolute Monarchy",
        situation: "You are the second Druk Gyalpo, the Dragon King, ruling a Himalayan realm that most of the world cannot find on a map. Bhutan has no roads, no army worth the name, no currency of its own, and almost no contact with outsiders; monasteries and district lords answer to your court at Bumthang. That isolation has been your shield. In August 1949 you signed a treaty with newly independent India: Delhi returned the Dewangiri tract, pays a subsidy, and pledges non-interference at home while guiding your hand abroad.\n\nThe bargain looks prudent, because the mountains no longer guarantee safety. Mao's revolution has triumphed in China, and Communist maps speak of Tibet, your cultural motherland and northern neighbor, as a lost province to be liberated. If the Chinese army crosses the high plateau, hermit kingdoms may find their seclusion is no defense at all. Your task is ancient and simple: keep the dragon's land intact, unnoticed, and free.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Guard the hidden Dragon Kingdom as giants close in.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("CAN"),
        display_name: "Dominion of Canada",
        leader_name: "Louis St. Laurent",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You lead a country quietly becoming a power. Newfoundland joined Confederation in 1949 as the tenth province, you were a founding signatory of the North Atlantic Treaty, and 'Uncle Louis' St. Laurent won his own sweeping mandate in June. The economy booms: American capital pours into oil, minerals and timber, immigrants arrive by the shipload, and the St. Lawrence Seaway sits on the drawing board.\n\nYour diplomats preach the new gospel of the middle power, bridging Washington and London while building up the United Nations. But the Cold War began, in a sense, in Ottawa — Igor Gouzenko's 1945 defection exposed Soviet espionage to the West — and geography puts you directly beneath the polar flight path of any future war. Quebec under Duplessis guards its autonomy jealously, and your small peacetime army seems adequate for a peaceable era. Should a distant conflict erupt this year, Canada's comfortable assumptions may be tested sooner than anyone expects.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The quiet middle power under the polar flight path.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("CHE"),
        display_name: "Swiss Confederation",
        leader_name: "Max Petitpierre",
        leader_title: "President of the Confederation",
        government: "Directorial Democracy",
        situation: "You preside over the one nation in Europe the war never touched. While every neighbour dug out of rubble, Switzerland emerged with its cities whole, its banks full, and its franc among the hardest currencies on earth. Max Petitpierre, foreign minister and this year's President of the rotating Federal Council, has given the old neutrality a new doctrine: 'neutrality and solidarity' — no alliances, no United Nations membership, but good offices, humanitarian work, and quiet usefulness to all sides. The citizen army in its Alpine redoubt makes any invader's arithmetic grim.\n\nBut the Cold War strains neutrality as fascism never quite did. Washington presses you to join export controls against the East; Moscow calls your banking discretion a bourgeois conspiracy; and wartime dealings in gold have left uncomfortable questions on the ledger. Prosperity is assured — Geneva hums with diplomats, Zurich with capital. The harder task is proving that in a world dividing into two armed camps, there is still honour, profit, and safety in belonging to neither.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Armed neutrality in a world choosing sides.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("CHL"),
        display_name: "Republic of Chile",
        leader_name: "Gabriel González Videla",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Presidential Democracy",
        situation: "You won the presidency in 1946 with Communist votes and gave Communists cabinet seats — then turned on them utterly. The Law for the Permanent Defense of Democracy, which your enemies call the Ley Maldita, outlawed the party in 1948, struck thousands from the voter rolls, and filled the Pisagua detention camp in the northern desert with militants. Senator Pablo Neruda, who denounced you from the Senate floor, fled across the Andes on horseback into exile, and his poems now travel the world carrying your name as a curse.\n\nChile remains a constitutional republic — elections, congress, courts all function — but the coalition politics beneath you are treacherous, and the copper that pays for everything belongs to American companies whose taxes you must bargain for dollar by dollar. Inflation grinds the salaried classes, strikes ripple through the mines and cities, and the banned left organizes in the shadows. Hold the center of a fractious democracy while the Cold War demands you choose sides ever more loudly.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Copper, poets, and a banned party underground.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("COL"),
        display_name: "Republic of Colombia",
        leader_name: "Mariano Ospina Pérez",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Conservative Authoritarian Republic",
        situation: "Your country is bleeding. Since the assassination of Jorge Eliécer Gaitán in April 1948 set Bogotá ablaze, La Violencia has spread through the coffee highlands and llanos: Conservative police and Liberal guerrillas murder each other village by village, and the dead already number in the tens of thousands. President Mariano Ospina Pérez shuttered Congress at gunpoint in November 1949 and governs under state of siege. Laureano Gómez, the fiery Conservative ideologue whom the Liberals refused even to run against, won November's uncontested election and waits to take power in August.\n\nCoffee prices are strong and Washington counts you a firm friend against communism, but the state's writ barely runs beyond the cities. Liberal bands are organizing in the eastern plains, the army's loyalty is bought with a free hand in the countryside, and Gómez dreams of remaking the republic along corporatist lines. Whether you crush the insurgency, negotiate peace, or drown in partisan blood, 1950 will decide what kind of Colombia survives.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "End La Violencia — or win it.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("CRI"),
        display_name: "Republic of Costa Rica",
        leader_name: "Otilio Ulate Blanco",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Presidential Democracy",
        situation: "You govern a republic that has just done the unthinkable: it abolished its own army. Two years ago a stolen election plunged Costa Rica into civil war, and the coffee farmer José Figueres shot his way to power in forty days. Then came the astonishments — his junta dissolved the military and turned the Bellavista barracks into a museum, taxed the rich, nationalized the banks, and in November 1949 calmly handed the presidency to Otilio Ulate, the very man whose election victory had been annulled. A new constitution enfranchises women and Afro-Costa Ricans and stakes everything on schools instead of soldiers.\n\nBut disarmament is a wager, not a guarantee. The defeated calderonistas brood in exile under Somoza's wing in Nicaragua, and they have invaded once already. Figueres, restless on his farm, plainly intends to be president himself one day. You must prove that a small democracy with no army, guarded only by law and the goodwill of neighbors who wish you dead, can survive the Cold War.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "A republic that dared to disband its army.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("CSK"),
        display_name: "Czechoslovak Republic",
        leader_name: "Klement Gottwald",
        leader_title: "President and KSČ Chairman",
        government: "Communist One-Party State",
        situation: "You are Klement Gottwald, and you took Czechoslovakia without a shot. The February 1948 coup handed your Communist Party total power in the only industrialized democracy east of the Iron Curtain; Jan Masaryk fell dead beneath his ministry window weeks later, and President Beneš, broken, died before the year was out. Now you sit in Prague Castle ruling the arsenal of the Eastern Bloc — Škoda's works, machine tools, and the uranium of Jáchymov, mined by prisoners to feed Stalin's bomb.\n\nBut power has brought paranoia. The Rajk trial in Budapest set the template, and Soviet advisers now comb your own party for the conspiracy Stalin insists must exist. The Church is being strangled, monasteries emptied, borders sealed with wire. Show trials of class enemies fill the courts, and the list of suspects grows toward men who stood beside you in February. The purge you unleashed is still hungry, and it does not care who built it.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The purge you started is coming for your friends.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("CUB"),
        display_name: "Republic of Cuba",
        leader_name: "Carlos Prío Socarrás",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Presidential Democracy",
        situation: "You govern the richest island in the Caribbean at the height of its glittering, rotten prosperity. Sugar money floods Havana; American tourists pack the casinos; a new national bank and ambitious public works promise modernity. President Prío, the 'cordial president,' heir of the Auténtico movement born in the 1933 revolution, presides over a genuine democracy — and over a state hollowed out by graft, where ministries leak millions and armed 'action groups' settle political scores with tommy guns in the streets.\n\nEvery Sunday night the radio crackles with Eddy Chibás, the incorruptible crusader whose Ortodoxo party is turning your government's scandals into a national morality play before the 1952 elections. And in the Senate sits a watchful former sergeant named Fulgencio Batista, back from Florida, remembering how easily Cuban democracy once fell to a man with the army behind him. The republic looks unshakeable from the Malecón. It is not. What you tolerate now will decide what Cuba becomes.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Sugar, gangsters, and a sergeant waiting offstage.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("DNK"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Denmark",
        leader_name: "Hans Hedtoft",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "You govern the Kingdom of Denmark, a small nation that learned a bitter lesson in April 1940, when German troops overran the country in a single morning. Neutrality failed. Last April, Prime Minister Hans Hedtoft led Denmark into the Atlantic Pact despite deep misgivings at home, binding the kingdom to the West. Your Social Democratic minority government must now balance the rearmament your new allies demand against a fragile economy, still rationed and shaken by September's devaluation of the krone.\n\nDenmark's reach stretches far beyond Jutland. Greenland, vast and empty, has become priceless real estate in an age of polar bombers, and Washington's interest in its icefields grows by the month. The Baltic straits your modest navy guards are now NATO's front gate, with Soviet forces just across the water. You must arm without going bankrupt, ally without disappearing, and decide how much of the Arctic you are willing to share.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Guard NATO's Baltic gate — and Greenland's frozen prize.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("DOM"),
        display_name: "Dominican Republic",
        leader_name: "Rafael Leónidas Trujillo",
        leader_title: "President and Generalissimo",
        government: "Personalist Dictatorship",
        situation: "You are the Benefactor of the Fatherland, and after twenty years there is nothing in the Dominican Republic you do not own, oversee, or overhear. The capital bears your name, churches post the motto 'God and Trujillo,' and your sugar mills, cattle ranches, and monopolies have made your family the state itself. Your secret police reach into every village and across every sea; exiles who speak too loudly have a way of dying in foreign cities. Order, roads, and a paid-off foreign debt are your boast — terror is your method.\n\nBut the neighborhood has turned hostile. Last June the Caribbean Legion's exiles splashed ashore at Luperón in a doomed seaborne raid, blessed by your enemies Arévalo in Guatemala and the Cubans. You answer conspiracy with conspiracy, plotting against Havana and San José while draping yourself in the banner of anti-communism, betting that Washington's fear of Moscow outweighs its distaste for you. The Cold War may yet make your tyranny respectable. Nothing, however, makes it safe.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The Benefactor tolerates no rivals, anywhere.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ECU"),
        display_name: "Republic of Ecuador",
        leader_name: "Galo Plaza Lasso",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Presidential Democracy",
        situation: "You are the rarest thing in Ecuadorian politics: a president who might actually finish his term. Galo Plaza Lasso, hacienda-born but Cornell-educated, took office in 1948 promising honest administration and development over caudillo drama, in a country that burned through a dozen governments in the previous two decades. But August 1949 brought catastrophe — the Ambato earthquake flattened towns across the central highlands, killed more than five thousand people, and left reconstruction hanging over your budget as the new year opens.\n\nHope grows in the coastal lowlands, where banana plantations are expanding fast and foreign buyers are hungry; the boom could transform your export economy if roads and ports keep pace. The wound that will not close is the 1941 war with Peru and the Rio Protocol that stripped away your Amazonian claims — no politician can accept it aloud, and the ex-president Velasco Ibarra waits abroad for democracy to stumble. Keep the economy growing and the barracks quiet, and you may yet prove constitutional government can take root.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Bananas, earthquakes, and a democracy worth keeping.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("EGY"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Egypt",
        leader_name: "Farouk I",
        leader_title: "King",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "You are Farouk, King of Egypt and the Sudan, not yet thirty and already going to seed in the eyes of your subjects. The catastrophe in Palestine — defective arms, defeated armies, the humiliation of the Faluja pocket — is laid at your palace door. Prime Minister Nuqrashi was assassinated by the Muslim Brotherhood; the Brotherhood's own Supreme Guide, Hassan al-Banna, was gunned down in reply. Now you have gambled on legitimacy: free elections on January 3rd, which the Wafd of your old enemy Mustafa al-Nahhas will surely win.\n\nBeyond the palace gates, tens of thousands of British soldiers still garrison the Suez Canal Zone under a treaty every Egyptian despises, and evacuation is the cry of every party. You must ride the Wafd, the Brotherhood, the communists, and the street's fury at Britain — while in the officers' clubs, young veterans of Palestine meet quietly and take notes on everything the old order does wrong.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "The Wafd rises, the British linger, the officers whisper.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ESP"),
        display_name: "Spanish State",
        leader_name: "Francisco Franco",
        leader_title: "Caudillo",
        government: "Personalist Dictatorship",
        situation: "You rule as Caudillo by the grace of God — and by the memory of a civil war whose graves are barely a decade old. The victorious democracies treat you as fascism's last orphan: the United Nations has recalled its ambassadors, the Marshall Plan's bounty flows everywhere but Spain, and the borders stay half-closed. At home, autarky has bred the 'years of hunger' — ration cards, black markets, and a countryside still policed against maquis guerrillas. The monarchists press you on the succession you promised in 1947 but refuse to schedule; Don Juan waits in Estoril.\n\nYet the world is turning your way. As the Cold War hardens, American admirals eye your ports and airfields, and anti-communism — your creed since 1936 — is suddenly the West's currency. Play the hermit fortress or the indispensable sentinel of the Mediterranean; outlast the ostracism, feed your people, and make the democracies come knocking. Franco has never hurried for anyone. Vigilance, patience, and the Guardia Civil sustain the regime.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Outlast isolation; make the free world need Franco.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ETH"),
        display_name: "Empire of Ethiopia",
        leader_name: "Haile Selassie I",
        leader_title: "Emperor",
        government: "Absolute Monarchy",
        situation: "You are restored. Five years of Italian occupation ended in 1941 when you returned to Addis Ababa behind Allied armies, and you have spent the decade since rebuilding an ancient empire with modern tools: foreign advisers, a new currency, schools, the beginnings of an air force. But Ethiopia remains a land of feudal lords and provincial armies, and your centralizing reforms grind against centuries of custom. British influence lingers where you would prefer American friendship — and American arms.\n\nTo the north lies the prize: Eritrea, the former Italian colony whose fate the United Nations will decide this year. A UN commission is coming; unionists agitate, separatists resist, and shifta bandits burn the countryside. Win Eritrea and the empire gains a Red Sea coast at last. Your standing as an ancient Christian kingdom and the League of Nations' most famous victim gives you moral capital abroad. Spend it shrewdly — and watch for distant wars where a small contribution might purchase great friendships.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Win Eritrea and give the Lion of Judah a coastline.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("FIN"),
        display_name: "Republic of Finland",
        leader_name: "Juho Kusti Paasikivi",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You are Juho Kusti Paasikivi, the aged president who has staked Finland's survival on a single hard truth: Moscow must never again see Finland as a threat. You fought the Soviets twice and lost Karelia, resettling four hundred thousand refugees. War reparation trains still roll east on schedule, and Soviet guns at the leased Porkkala base point at Helsinki itself. The 1948 Treaty of Friendship binds you to the USSR — but you negotiated its limits line by line, keeping Finnish democracy alive where every other Soviet neighbor fell.\n\nYou refused Marshall aid rather than provoke the Kremlin, yet ejected the communists from cabinet in 1948 and broke their violent strikes since. Fagerholm's Social Democratic minority government holds the home front while you conduct the delicate eastern policy that bears your name. A presidential election comes within weeks, and ambitious men like Urho Kekkonen circle. One misstep on this tightrope and forty years of independence could end.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Walk the tightrope between Moscow and the West.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("FRA"),
        display_name: "Fourth French Republic",
        leader_name: "Georges Bidault",
        leader_title: "President of the Council of Ministers",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You preside over a republic reborn from occupation and shame, and already wobbling. Georges Bidault's cabinet is the sixth since liberation; the National Assembly devours governments, the Communists command a quarter of the electorate and the great unions, while de Gaulle's RPF thunders against the regime itself from the right. Marshall dollars and Monnet's planners are rebuilding shattered industry, but inflation and strikes gnaw at every gain.\n\nAbroad, the tricolore is under siege. In Indochina, the war against Ho Chi Minh's Viet Minh grows dearer in blood and francs each month — and Mao's victorious China now stands at Tonkin's border, ready to arm your enemy. North Africa stirs. Yet France also holds Europe's future in its hands: bold minds in the Quai d'Orsay dream of binding German coal and French steel so tightly that war becomes impossible. Reconciliation or revanche, empire or Europe — the Fourth Republic must choose, if it survives long enough to do so.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "Hold Indochina, tame the Assembly, invent Europe.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("FRG"),
        display_name: "Federal Republic of Germany",
        leader_name: "Konrad Adenauer",
        leader_title: "Federal Chancellor",
        government: "Occupied Democracy",
        situation: "You lead a state that is barely seven months old, built on the ruins of the Reich. Konrad Adenauer, elected Chancellor by a single vote — his own — governs from provincial Bonn under the watchful eyes of the Allied High Commission. The Occupation Statute limits your sovereignty; the Ruhr's industry answers to an international authority; you have no army, no foreign ministry, and eight million refugees from the lost East crowding your cities. Across the Elbe, the Soviets have raised a rival German state that claims your future.\n\nYet the November Petersberg Agreement has already loosened the dismantling of your factories, and the currency reform has ignited an economic stirring that Ludwig Erhard promises will become a miracle. Adenauer's wager is audacious: bind Germany so firmly to the West that the occupiers become allies and sovereignty returns piece by piece. Coal, steel, and patience are your weapons. Rearmament is unspeakable today — but the Cold War may yet make the unspeakable necessary.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "From rubble to redemption, one concession at a time.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("GBR"),
        display_name: "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland",
        leader_name: "Clement Attlee",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You govern a victorious empire that can no longer afford its victory. Clement Attlee's Labour government has built a New Jerusalem at home — the National Health Service, nationalised coal, rail and steel — but the bill has come due. Rationing persists five years after the war, September's humiliating devaluation cut the pound from $4.03 to $2.80, and American loans keep the lights on. India is gone, Palestine abandoned, and a communist insurgency burns through the rubber plantations of Malaya.\n\nYet Britain remains a great power in every ledger that matters: a founding pillar of NATO, a permanent seat on the Security Council, and a secret atomic bomb programme racing toward its first test. A general election looms within weeks, and Churchill waits hungrily in opposition. Can you renew the socialist experiment, hold the sterling area together, and keep Britain at the top table — or will the cost of empire and welfare together break the exchequer?",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "Manage decline — or forge a second British century.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("GDR"),
        display_name: "German Democratic Republic",
        leader_name: "Walter Ulbricht",
        leader_title: "Deputy Minister-President and de facto SED leader",
        government: "Communist One-Party State",
        situation: "Your state is twelve weeks old. The German Democratic Republic was proclaimed on October 7th, 1949, in the Soviet zone of a divided Germany; old Wilhelm Pieck holds the presidency and Grotewohl the premiership, but you, Walter Ulbricht, control the party machine — and in this state, the party is everything. Above you all stands the Soviet Control Commission, and Soviet reparations crews are still unbolting entire factories and shipping them east. Bonn calls itself the only Germany and calls you a puppet.\n\nBerlin, deep inside your territory, remains a divided city with an open border through which your discontented citizens simply walk west — a hemorrhage of workers and engineers you cannot yet stop. The SED is being purged into a disciplined party of the new type, and plans for a new ministry of state security are taking shape. You must build socialism in half a country, with the other half watching, broadcasting, and waiting for you to fail.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Build socialism in half a Germany.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("GRC"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Greece",
        leader_name: "Alexandros Diomidis",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "The guns finally fell silent in October. After three years of civil war, the communist Democratic Army was broken on the peaks of Grammos and Vitsi and its remnants fled across the northern border — a defeat sealed when Tito, feuding with Stalin, closed Yugoslavia to them. Greece has won, but at a terrible price: tens of thousands dead atop the wartime ruin, seven hundred thousand villagers displaced, prisoners crowding the camps of Makronisos, and thousands of children carried off to the communist north.\n\nYou are Alexandros Diomidis, the elderly banker who inherited the premiership when Sophoulis died in June, presiding over a quarrelsome Liberal–Populist coalition that is visibly coming apart. American dollars pour in under the Truman and Marshall programs, and American advisers expect results. King Paul watches from the palace, the victorious Marshal Papagos from the army, and elections cannot be delayed much longer. Reconstruction or reprisal, mercy or vigilance — a drained nation waits to see which Greece you will build.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Rebuild a kingdom from civil war's ashes.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("GTM"),
        display_name: "Republic of Guatemala",
        leader_name: "Juan José Arévalo",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Reformist Democracy",
        situation: "You govern the boldest experiment in Central America. Since the October Revolution of 1944 swept away the dictator Ubico, President Arévalo's 'spiritual socialism' has given Guatemala a labor code, social security, free elections, and a taste of dignity for the Maya majority. It has also earned you some two dozen coup attempts and the cold fury of the United Fruit Company, whose plantations, railroad, and port give it a stranglehold on your economy — and friends in Washington.\n\nThe revolution nearly devoured itself in July, when army chief Francisco Arana was gunned down at Puente de la Gloria and his partisans rose in revolt. Defense Minister Jacobo Árbenz crushed the uprising, and now stands as the revolution's armed heir and the leading candidate for November's presidential election. Push reform deeper and you invite the label of communism; retreat and you betray everything 1944 promised. The Ten Years of Spring have reached their most dangerous season.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Ten years of spring, if you can keep them.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("HND"),
        display_name: "Republic of Honduras",
        leader_name: "Juan Manuel Gálvez",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Oligarchic Republic",
        situation: "You govern the original banana republic. For sixteen years the caudillo Tiburcio Carías ruled Honduras with jail cells and exile; a year ago he handed the sash to his chosen successor, Juan Manuel Gálvez — a mild-mannered lawyer who spent decades on the payroll of the United Fruit Company. The fruit companies own the north-coast plantations, the railroads, and the ports, and pay almost nothing in tax. Yet Gálvez is quietly surprising everyone: press censorship eases, exiles trickle home, roads and schools appear, and a central bank is taking shape.\n\nYour poverty is your protection and your prison. Honduras has little industry, few paved roads, and an army loyal mainly to whoever pays it. Next door, Guatemala's revolution radiates dangerous ideas about labor rights that your banana workers can hear across the border. Whether cautious liberalization can outrun the demands rising from the plantations — and the plotting of Carías's impatient old guard — will define the decade.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The banana republic learns to breathe again.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("HTI"),
        display_name: "Republic of Haiti",
        leader_name: "Dumarsais Estimé",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Presidential Republic",
        situation: "You govern the world's first black republic, and for once its president looks like its people. The Revolution of 1946 broke the mulatto elite's grip on power and raised Dumarsais Estimé, a schoolteacher's son from the Artibonite, to the National Palace. You have given Haiti income taxes on the rich, recognition of Vodou culture, new schools, and the dazzling Bicentennial Exposition now lighting up the Port-au-Prince waterfront — a world's fair meant to announce a new Haiti, built at staggering cost to a threadbare treasury.\n\nBut the pillars of your power are cracking. The elite despises you, the American fruit interests distrust you, and the Senate blocks your reforms at every turn. Your loyalists whisper that only a constitutional amendment — and a longer term — can save the revolution; your enemies call that dictatorship. Above it all stands the army of Colonel Paul Magloire, the same officers who installed you in 1946 and have never forgotten that what the barracks gives, the barracks can take away.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The black republic's revolution hangs by a thread.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("HUN"),
        display_name: "Hungarian People's Republic",
        leader_name: "Mátyás Rákosi",
        leader_title: "General Secretary, Hungarian Working People's Party",
        government: "Stalinist Dictatorship",
        situation: "You are Mátyás Rákosi, and you call yourself Stalin's best Hungarian pupil. By salami tactics you sliced away every rival party until the People's Republic was proclaimed in August 1949 under a constitution copied from Moscow's. Cardinal Mindszenty rots in prison after a drugged confession. László Rajk, your own foreign minister, hanged in October after a show trial you scripted personally — a warning to Tito's sympathizers and to everyone else. The ÁVH secret police watch every factory, parish and kitchen table.\n\nThis very day, January 1st, your First Five-Year Plan takes effect: Hungary, a nation of farmers, is to become a country of iron and steel by sheer force of will. Peasants are being driven into collectives, class enemies deported from their homes, and living standards squeezed to feed the furnaces. Yugoslavia glowers across the southern border you have fortified. Obedience is total, and beneath it something is quietly beginning to smolder.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Forge a country of iron and steel — by terror.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("IDN"),
        display_name: "United States of Indonesia",
        leader_name: "Sukarno",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Federal Parliamentary Republic",
        situation: "Five days ago, on December 27, the Dutch finally signed away the Indies, and the revolution you proclaimed in 1945 stands victorious after four years of war, two colonial offensives, and your own capture at Yogyakarta. You are Bung Karno, voice of merdeka, president of seventeen thousand islands. But victory came with strings: the Round Table gave you a federal state stitched from Dutch-made puppet republics, saddled you with Dutch debts, and kept West New Guinea in colonial hands. Vice President Hatta manages the cabinet; you embody the nation.\n\nAlready the federal structure groans, as region after region demands merger into the unitary Republic. Darul Islam rebels fight for an Islamic state in the West Java hills, demobilized guerrillas roam armed, and embittered Dutch colonial soldiers of fortune whisper of coups. The Communists, bloodied at Madiun in 1948, are rebuilding quietly. You must weld one nation from a thousand pieces before someone tears it apart.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Independent five days, seventeen thousand islands, one chance.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("IND"),
        display_name: "Dominion of India",
        leader_name: "Jawaharlal Nehru",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You govern a giant still bleeding from its birth. Partition in 1947 tore away Pakistan and set millions of refugees marching through massacre; the war it sparked over Kashmir froze into an uneasy ceasefire line only a year ago. Since then you have stitched together over five hundred princely states, marched troops into Hyderabad, and drafted the longest constitution on earth. On January 26 the Dominion dies and the Republic of India is born, with elections to follow that will be the largest act of democracy in human history.\n\nYet the world will not wait for your experiment. Mao's armies have just triumphed next door, and you were among the first to recognize Red China, betting that friendship can tame revolution. Washington and Moscow both demand you choose a side; you intend to choose neither. With famine, poverty, and Pakistan's grievance ever present, your non-aligned path must prove that a poor, free nation can stand on its own.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "Forge the world's largest democracy from partition's ashes.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("IRL"),
        display_name: "Republic of Ireland",
        leader_name: "John A. Costello",
        leader_title: "Taoiseach",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You lead a republic not yet a year old. On Easter Monday 1949, John A. Costello's inter-party government — an improbable coalition spanning Fine Gael to republicans — severed the last link to the British Crown and took Ireland out of the Commonwealth, completing what 1916 began. But the republic stops at the border: six counties remain under British rule, and your government has made anti-partition its foreign policy's beating heart, even refusing to join NATO while Ireland stays divided. Neutrality is again your shield, as it was through the war.\n\nAt home the picture is harder. The economy stagnates behind tariff walls; emigrant ships carry away the young to Britain and America in their tens of thousands; tuberculosis stalks the poor, though an energetic young health minister, Noel Browne, is emptying the sanatoria. Church and state coexist in an embrace that may yet tighten into conflict over who commands Ireland's social policy. A proud new flag flies — now give the republic an economy and a future worthy of it.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "A new republic, an unfinished island.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("IRN"),
        display_name: "Imperial State of Iran",
        leader_name: "Mohammad Reza Pahlavi",
        leader_title: "Shah",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "You are Mohammad Reza Pahlavi, thirty years old, Shah of an ancient land squeezed between the Soviet Union and the British Empire. A gunman nearly killed you at Tehran University in February 1949; you answered by banning the communist Tudeh Party and summoning a Constituent Assembly that granted you the power to dissolve the Majlis. The Red Army quit your northern provinces in 1946 only under Western pressure, and Moscow has not forgotten the humiliation.\n\nThe real battle is oil. The Anglo-Iranian Oil Company takes the lion's share of Abadan's profits while Iranian workers live in slums, and the Supplemental Agreement meant to soothe the country sits stalled in a hostile Majlis. Mohammad Mosaddegh's new National Front cries fraud over the elections and demands nationalization outright. You dream of a modern army and reform from above, but between the mullahs, the communists, and the British, the throne you inherited at twenty-one has never felt narrower.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Your oil, their empire. The Majlis grows restless.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("IRQ"),
        display_name: "Hashemite Kingdom of Iraq",
        leader_name: "Abd al-Ilah",
        leader_title: "Regent",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "Your king is a boy: Faisal II, fourteen years old and away at school in England, while you, Crown Prince Abd al-Ilah, rule as Regent from Baghdad. Behind you stands the old fox Nuri al-Said and a narrow circle of politicians who rotate the premiership among themselves, and behind them Britain, whose treaty rights and air bases underwrite the Hashemite order. The Wathbah of 1948, the bloody street risings that killed the Portsmouth Treaty, showed how thin that crust really is.\n\nDefeat in Palestine still burns. Your army came home from a war the politicians could not win, and the street blames the throne. Oil royalties from Kirkuk promise development money if pipelines and politics allow, while agitation swirls around Iraq's ancient Jewish community and communists organize in secret. Faisal comes of age in 1953; until then every riot, every conspiracy, and every quarrel with Cairo or Damascus over Arab leadership lands on your desk.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Guard the boy king until 1953 — if you can.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ISL"),
        display_name: "Republic of Iceland",
        leader_name: "Ólafur Thors",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You lead Iceland, a republic only since 1944 and a nation with no army at all — not one soldier. Yet geography has made your island of one hundred forty thousand people one of the most courted places on Earth: whoever holds Iceland commands the North Atlantic air and sea lanes. Last March you joined the Atlantic Pact while tear gas drifted over the Althing and protesters hurled stones, and the Americans retain rights at the Keflavík airfield they built during the war.\n\nEverything else depends on fish. Cod is your currency, your diplomacy, and your future, and your fleets already chafe against British trawlers working grounds you consider your own. At home, Ólafur Thors's Independence Party governs alone in a fragile minority cabinet formed only weeks ago in December, facing runaway inflation and a currency crisis that demands painful medicine. You must sell the fish, tame the prices, and decide what price the superpowers should pay for your rock in the ocean.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "No army, one airbase, and all the cod.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ISR"),
        display_name: "State of Israel",
        leader_name: "David Ben-Gurion",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You are David Ben-Gurion, and twenty months ago you proclaimed a state that five Arab armies marched to strangle. Israel survived; the 1949 armistice lines leave you more land than the UN partition offered, but no peace, no recognized borders, and hundreds of thousands of Palestinian refugees encamped just beyond them. In December, after the UN voted to internationalize Jerusalem, you defiantly began moving your government to the city.\n\nNow the second war begins: absorption. Immigrants pour in — survivors from Europe's camps, entire communities airlifted from Yemen, soon perhaps from Iraq — doubling your population and swamping the economy. Rationing governs every meal under the austerity regime, tent camps ring the cities, and you must build an army, a water system, and a nation simultaneously. The great powers offer sympathy but few arms, and the Arab states refuse to turn armistice into peace. Everything about this state is provisional except your determination.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "A newborn state, doubled by exodus, ringed by enemies.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ITA"),
        display_name: "Italian Republic",
        leader_name: "Alcide De Gasperi",
        leader_title: "President of the Council of Ministers",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You steer a young republic balanced on a knife's edge between Washington and Moscow. Alcide De Gasperi's Christian Democrats crushed the left in the dramatic 1948 election, and Italy has since joined NATO and drunk deeply of Marshall aid — yet Palmiro Togliatti's Communists remain the largest such party in the Western world, entrenched in the factories of Turin and the villages of Emilia. Every strike, every land occupation in the impoverished South, tests whether ballots or barricades will decide Italy's fate.\n\nThe war's scars are still raw: Trieste remains under Anglo-American occupation, claimed by Tito's Yugoslavia; the Mezzogiorno seethes with landless peasants demanding reform De Gasperi has promised but not yet delivered. Industry revives in the North while millions look to emigrate. The Vatican watches over your shoulder, the Americans over the other. Hold the centre, reform fast enough to steal the revolution's thunder, and Italy may become Europe's success story — falter, and the Cold War's front line runs through Rome.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Keep the West's largest Communist Party at bay.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("JAP"),
        display_name: "Japan",
        leader_name: "Shigeru Yoshida",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Occupied Democracy",
        situation: "You lead a defeated nation that is no longer fully its own. American occupation authorities under General MacArthur retain supreme power, though they govern through your cabinet and have imposed a pacifist constitution that renounces war itself. The harsh Dodge Line austerity has strangled inflation at the cost of bankruptcies, layoffs, and seething labor unrest, much of it communist-led. Your cities remain scarred by firebombing, your empire is gone, and your trade lies at the occupier's mercy.\n\nYet opportunity glimmers. Washington's priorities are shifting from punishing Japan to rebuilding it as the anchor of a free Asia, and talk of a peace treaty grows louder. You believe recovery must come before rearmament, prosperity before pride. Navigate between American demands, leftist agitation, and nationalist resentment, and Japan may regain its sovereignty sooner than anyone dreams. Events on the nearby Korean peninsula may prove unexpectedly decisive for your economy and your future.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Rebuild a nation from occupation's shadow.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("JOR"),
        display_name: "Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan",
        leader_name: "Abdullah I",
        leader_title: "King",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "You are Abdullah, son of the Sharif of Mecca, King of the newly renamed Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan. Alone among the Arab rulers, you emerged from the Palestine war stronger: your British-officered Arab Legion, the finest army in the Arab world, holds the West Bank and the Old City of Jerusalem with its holy places. The Jericho Conference has already acclaimed you sovereign of both banks, and elections in April promise to seal a formal union — an annexation the Arab League denounces as theft.\n\nYou have never hidden your ambition for a Greater Syria under Hashemite crowns, nor entirely hidden your quiet conversations with the Israelis about a settlement no other Arab leader dares discuss. Half a million embittered Palestinian refugees are now your subjects, and many curse your name in the mosques. Between Cairo's hostility, London's subsidies, and the whispered threats of assassins, you play the oldest game in the desert: survival through audacity.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Annex the West Bank — then survive what follows.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("KOR"),
        display_name: "Republic of Korea",
        leader_name: "Syngman Rhee",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Authoritarian Presidential Republic",
        situation: "You are seventy-four, a lifelong exile turned founding president, and your Republic of Korea holds the poorer half of a divided peninsula. The Japanese left in 1945; the Americans, who midwifed your state, withdrew their last combat troops in June 1949, leaving you a lightly armed force without tanks, heavy artillery, or combat aircraft. Communist guerrillas smolder in the southern mountains, the legacy of bloody uprisings on Jeju and at Yosu, and border firefights flare along the 38th parallel.\n\nYou speak loudly of marching north to unify Korea, which is precisely why Washington keeps your army weak. Inflation ravages the cities, land reform is unfinished, and May's National Assembly elections may humble you. Above the parallel, Kim Il-sung commands a Soviet-equipped army that outmatches yours in every category, and American officials publicly hesitate to place Korea within their defensive perimeter. Hold your republic together, court your distracted patron, and watch the north. This year will test everything.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "Hold the 38th parallel.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("LBN"),
        display_name: "Republic of Lebanon",
        leader_name: "Bechara El Khoury",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Confessional Parliamentary Republic",
        situation: "You govern a republic built on an unwritten bargain. As President Bechara El Khoury you embody the National Pact of 1943: a Maronite president, a Sunni prime minister — your indispensable partner Riad al-Solh — and power parceled among the sects in careful ratios. Independence from France is won, and Beirut is booming: gold, banking, and transit trade flow through your port, the Arab world's window to the West.\n\nBut 1949 tested the bargain. Antun Saadeh's Syrian nationalists attempted revolt; you crushed them and had Saadeh shot after a summary trial, earning blood feuds that may yet find your ministers. Armistice with Israel closed the southern front, yet a hundred thousand Palestinian refugees now camp on your soil, an unresolved weight in the confessional arithmetic. Across the eastern border Syria devours its governments in coups, while critics at home mutter about the amended constitution that kept you in office and the fortunes gathering around your family. Lebanon is prosperity and fragility at once.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Balance the sects, bank the region's riches.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("LBR"),
        display_name: "Republic of Liberia",
        leader_name: "William V. S. Tubman",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Dominant-Party Republic",
        situation: "Africa's oldest republic has new energy. President since 1944, you have thrown the doors open: Firestone's million-acre rubber plantation anchors the economy, the US dollar circulates as legal tender, and the American-built Free Port of Monrovia and Roberts Field tie you to Washington. Iron ore at Bomi Hills promises the country's first mining boom, and your Open Door Policy welcomes any investor willing to pay.\n\nYet Liberia is two nations. The Americo-Liberian elite of the coast — descendants of freed American slaves — rules an indigenous interior majority that cannot vote, and your True Whig Party has held power for three-quarters of a century through patronage and loyalty. Your Unification Policy promises to close the gap, at your own careful pace. As colonial Africa begins to stir, your century of independence makes you both symbol and anomaly, and America's Cold War interest in your rubber and your coastline is at once your shield and your leash.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Africa's oldest republic opens its doors to American millions.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("LIE"),
        display_name: "Principality of Liechtenstein",
        leader_name: "Franz Joseph II",
        leader_title: "Sovereign Prince",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "Franz Joseph II is the first sovereign prince ever to live among his people in Vaduz, and he has already faced down Stalin: in 1945 Liechtenstein sheltered five hundred soldiers of the First Russian National Army and refused Soviet demands for their repatriation — almost alone in Europe. Neutral through the war, bound to Switzerland by customs union and the Swiss franc, the principality survived intact while every neighbour burned.\n\nBut survival is not prosperity. Czechoslovakia and Poland have confiscated the princely family's vast estates without compensation, and the treasury is so thin the Prince quietly sells family art treasures to stay solvent. The farms of this alpine sliver cannot support its 13,000 people, so you gamble on transformation: precision industry, low taxes, and holding companies registering in Vaduz. Unarmed between Switzerland and Austria, you have no soldiers to spend and no ambitions of conquest. Your victory condition is different: turn a poor mountain principality into something the world's money cannot ignore.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The prince who told Stalin no builds a vault.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("LKA"),
        display_name: "Dominion of Ceylon",
        leader_name: "Don Stephen Senanayake",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You won independence in February 1948 without a shot fired, a rarity in Asia, and you govern the island the way you farmed it: patiently. Ceylon lives on tea, rubber, and coconut sold to the world, and British warships still call at Trincomalee under agreements you signed willingly. This month the Commonwealth's foreign ministers gather in Colombo, and the aid plan likely to bear your capital's name could make this small dominion the diplomatic center of free Asia.\n\nBeneath the calm, you have planted seeds whose harvest is uncertain. Your citizenship laws have stripped the vote from hundreds of thousands of Indian Tamil plantation workers, straining relations with Delhi and hardening communal lines. Marxist unions test you with strikes, and ambitious men in your own cabinet, including the restless Mr. Bandaranaike, weigh the politics of language and faith. Prosperity and parliamentary decency are your inheritance to defend; whether Sinhalese and Tamil can share the island's future is the question you dare not answer wrongly.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Asia's quiet success story, if the fault lines hold.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("LUX"),
        display_name: "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg",
        leader_name: "Pierre Dupong",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "You lead Europe's smallest sovereign player, and you have learned the hardest lesson twice. Neutrality did not stop the Kaiser in 1914 nor Hitler in 1940, when the Grand Duchy was annexed outright and its sons conscripted into the Wehrmacht. So in 1948 Luxembourg tore neutrality from its constitution, and Pierre Dupong — premier since 1937, companion of Grand Duchess Charlotte's government-in-exile — signed the North Atlantic Treaty as a founder. Never again will Luxembourg stand alone between France and Germany.\n\nYour weight far exceeds your size: the blast furnaces of ARBED make this nation of 300,000 one of the world's great steel producers per head, and steel is becoming the very language of European politics. In Benelux you helped invent economic union before the great powers dreamed of it. When France and Germany begin talking about pooling coal and steel, the smallest voice at the table will be among the most experienced. Prosperity, security, and a seat at every table — earn all three.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Steel gives the smallest nation a seat at every table.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("MCO"),
        display_name: "Principality of Monaco",
        leader_name: "Rainier III",
        leader_title: "Sovereign Prince",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "You are twenty-six years old and you have inherited a fading legend. Rainier III became sovereign when his grandfather Louis II died in May 1949; the formal investiture comes this April. Monte Carlo's casino, run by the Société des Bains de Mer, still pays the principality's bills, but the Belle Époque glamour has curdled — the tables are worn, the grand hotels half-empty, and the gambling monopoly that once made Monaco fabulous now barely sustains it.\n\nFrance surrounds you on every side, and under the treaty of 1918 your independence is conditional: should a prince die without an heir, Monaco's very sovereignty comes into question. The young prince's tasks are clear — revive the economy, draw new money to the harbour (a certain Greek shipowner is eyeing SBM shares), assert himself against a fractious National Council, and, in time, find a bride. Reinvent Monte Carlo for the modern age, or watch the principality fade into a French curiosity.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "A young prince must reinvent Monte Carlo or lose everything.",
    ),
