        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Dominant-Party Republic",
        situation: "You govern the great survivor of Latin America's revolutions. Miguel Alemán, the first civilian president of the post-revolutionary era, has traded cavalry charges for construction cranes: dams rise on the rivers, highways stitch the sierra together, and Ciudad Universitaria climbs out of the lava fields south of the capital. The Institutional Revolutionary Party controls every governorship and nearly every union, and its machine delivers elections with mechanical certainty. Business booms, and so does the fortune of everyone close to the president.\n\nYet the Revolution's old promises grow quiet. Land reform has slowed to a crawl, wages trail prices, and critics whisper that the cabinet has become a syndicate of forty thieves. Washington courts you as the indispensable southern neighbor, offering loans and markets while eyeing your nationalized oil with unconcealed hunger. War in Asia may soon send commodity prices soaring. Whether the boom builds a modern nation or merely a gilded elite is the question 1950 will begin to answer.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Industrialize the Revolution — and enrich its heirs.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("MMR"),
        display_name: "Union of Burma",
        leader_name: "U Nu",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You inherited a nation and it immediately caught fire. Aung San, the founding hero, was assassinated with half his cabinet in 1947, leaving you, a devout Buddhist writer, to lead. Within months of independence the Communists rose in the countryside, army battalions mutinied, and the Karen National Union stormed to within four miles of Rangoon, seizing Insein and Mandalay. Foreign wags called you the Rangoon government, sovereign over one embattled city. But through 1949 your loyal troops under General Ne Win clawed back the towns, and the Union, barely, endures.\n\nNow a new danger seeps across the northeastern frontier: defeated Chinese Nationalist soldiers, fleeing Mao's victory, are drifting into the Shan hills with their rifles and their opium. You have recognized Red China first among non-Communist nations, hoping candor buys safety, and you preach strict neutrality between the blocs. Rice exports must fund reconstruction, insurgents must be beaten or bought, and Buddhism must bind what war has torn.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Rangoon holds; now win back the rest of Burma.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("MNG"),
        display_name: "Mongolian People's Republic",
        leader_name: "Khorloogiin Choibalsan",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Communist One-Party State",
        situation: "You have ruled Mongolia for a decade as its Marshal and prime minister, Stalin's most faithful disciple in Asia. Yours was the world's second communist state, and it has paid the price of loyalty: the purges you conducted in the late 1930s destroyed the Buddhist clergy and took tens of thousands of lives. In return, Soviet backing secured formal independence, recognized by China after the 1945 plebiscite and confirmed in October, when you exchanged recognition with Mao's new People's Republic.\n\nNow your position grows delicate. With communist China and Soviet Russia clasping hands, Mongolia is no longer a contested buffer but a corridor between giants, and some in Beijing still murmur that Outer Mongolia is a lost province. Your economic plans push herders toward collectives and the young toward Russian schooling, while your protege Tsedenbal manages the party apparatus. Preserve your nation's fragile statehood between two colossal neighbors, and perhaps, in time, widen it.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Stalin's oldest satellite, wedged between giants.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("NIC"),
        display_name: "Republic of Nicaragua",
        leader_name: "Anastasio Somoza García",
        leader_title: "Chief Director of the National Guard (De Facto Ruler)",
        government: "Personalist Dictatorship",
        situation: "You are Tacho Somoza, and Nicaragua is the family business. Your elderly uncle Víctor Román y Reyes wears the presidential sash, but every rifle in the National Guard answers to you, as it has since you had Sandino shot in 1934. Your estates, factories, and shipping lines swallow a widening share of the economy; what the Guard does not control, the family owns. Washington once held you at arm's length for strangling democracy — now, with the Cold War hardening, an anti-communist strongman looks better every month.\n\nYour enemies multiply abroad. Figueres in Costa Rica and Arévalo in Guatemala bankroll the Caribbean Legion, exiles sworn to topple you, and you repay them by hosting their enemies in Managua. At home the Conservative opposition may finally be bought: a pact with old General Chamorro could dress your rule in constitutional clothes and return you to the presidency itself. The frail president cannot last forever. Nicaragua waits for you to stop pretending.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The Guard is yours. So is everything else.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("NLD"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of the Netherlands",
        leader_name: "Willem Drees",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "You govern a kingdom that lost an empire four days ago. On 27 December 1949, under relentless American pressure, Queen Juliana signed away the East Indies — three centuries of Dutch rule ended, and with it the wealth that once made this small nation great. Only West New Guinea remains in your hands, a thorn that will fester in relations with Sukarno's new Indonesia. Willem Drees, the austere social democrat whom pensioners call 'Father Drees', must now convince a bruised nation that its future lies in Europe, not the tropics.\n\nThat future is being built with remarkable discipline: wages are held down by national agreement, Marshall aid flows into dikes and factories, and the old neutrality is dead — the Netherlands has chosen NATO, Benelux, and every scheme of European cooperation on offer. Reconstruction from war and flood-scarred land proceeds house by house. Guide the emigration ships, the guilder, and the coalition; prosperity is close enough to touch, if the sea and history permit.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Rebuild below sea level, four days after losing an empire.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("NOR"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Norway",
        leader_name: "Einar Gerhardsen",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "You lead Norway, five years free of German occupation. Finnmark in the far north still lies in ashes where the retreating Wehrmacht burned every farm and pier, and reconstruction consumes everything your Labour government can muster. The voters renewed your majority in October 1949, endorsing the boldest decision of your career: abandoning a century of neutrality to become a founding member of the Atlantic Pact, over pointed diplomatic notes from Moscow.\n\nNo other NATO nation shares your predicament, for in the Arctic your border runs directly against the Soviet Union itself. To calm the Kremlin you have pledged that no foreign bases will stand on Norwegian soil in peacetime — a self-imposed restraint your allies accept uneasily. Your merchant fleet, among the world's largest, earns the currency reconstruction demands. Svalbard's coal islands, which Molotov once demanded to share, sit exposed in the polar sea. You must rebuild, rearm, and never provoke.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Share a border with Stalin — and hold it.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("NPL"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Nepal",
        leader_name: "Mohan Shamsher Jang Bahadur Rana",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Hereditary Autocracy",
        situation: "You are Maharaja Mohan Shamsher, hereditary Prime Minister, heir to the system Jung Bahadur built a century ago: the Ranas rule, the Shah kings reign as ornaments, and King Tribhuvan lives as a gilded captive in his own palace. Nepal remains closed, roadless, and yours, its Gurkha soldiers famous abroad while its people stay poor and unschooled at home. But the world that guaranteed your isolation is gone. The British have left India, and the democrats of Delhi have little love for a family autocracy on their border.\n\nExiles of the Nepali Congress gather in Indian cities, openly preaching revolution, and rumor says even the king listens. To the north, Mao's China reaches toward Tibet, making your mountain kingdom suddenly strategic. Nehru offers a treaty of peace and friendship whose price may be your independence of action. A century of Rana power now rests on how you handle one restless king and one impatient neighbor.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Keep the king caged and the century of Ranas alive.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("NZL"),
        display_name: "Dominion of New Zealand",
        leader_name: "Sidney Holland",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "Sidney Holland's National Party has just ended fourteen years of Labour government, winning November's election on a promise to dismantle wartime controls, end rationing, and let private enterprise breathe. His first target is constitutional: the appointed upper house will be abolished by a 'suicide squad' of new councillors voting their own chamber out of existence.\n\nProsperity rides on wool, meat and butter shipped to Britain, and any war scare would send wool prices soaring. But the militant watersiders' and miners' unions despise the new government, and a reckoning on the wharves is only a matter of time. Britain, exhausted, can no longer guarantee Pacific security, so like Australia you look increasingly to Washington. You govern a small, orderly, loyal dominion at the bottom of the world — yet the choices ahead, between empire and America, between confrontation and compromise with organized labour, will shape New Zealand for a generation.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "End rationing, break the wharves, ride the wool boom.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("OMN"),
        display_name: "Sultanate of Muscat and Oman",
        leader_name: "Said bin Taimur",
        leader_title: "Sultan",
        government: "Absolute Monarchy",
        situation: "You are Said bin Taimur, Sultan of Muscat and Oman since 1932, a meticulous, English-speaking recluse who inherited a bankrupt treasury and cleared its debts by spending almost nothing. Your writ runs along the coast from Muscat's twin forts; the mountainous interior obeys the Imam of Oman under the uneasy Treaty of Seeb, and the tribes acknowledge you only when it suits them. Britain supplies subsidy, advisers, and gunboats, and expects obedience in return.\n\nBeneath the deserts may lie oil. Petroleum concessions cover lands you do not actually control, and any push toward Buraimi or the interior will collide with the Imamate and with Saudi ambitions creeping east. You forbid radios, sunglasses, and schools beyond a handful, convinced that development breeds demands and demands breed revolt. Perhaps isolation preserves your throne another generation; perhaps it is a slow fuse. For now the sultanate is silent, solvent, and medieval — exactly as you have designed it.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "A silent sultanate, a rival Imam, oil beneath the sands.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("PAK"),
        display_name: "Dominion of Pakistan",
        leader_name: "Liaquat Ali Khan",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Democracy",
        situation: "You lead a state that experts said could not exist: two wings separated by a thousand miles of hostile India, born in 1947 with empty treasuries, borrowed typewriters, and eight million refugees. Jinnah, the Quaid-i-Azam, died in 1948, leaving you to finish what he began. The ceasefire in Kashmir holds, but the promised plebiscite does not come, and India controls the headwaters of the canals that feed your Punjab. Your Objectives Resolution has set the state on an Islamic foundation, yet a constitution remains unwritten and Bengal's voice grows restless.\n\nAbroad, Afghanistan agitates for a Pashtunistan carved from your frontier and cast the lone vote against your UN admission. You need patrons, arms, and wheat; both Washington and Moscow have extended invitations, and which capital you visit first may shape your destiny for decades. Everything about Pakistan is unfinished, which means everything is still possible.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Two wings, one faith, and Kashmir unfinished.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("PAN"),
        display_name: "Republic of Panama",
        leader_name: "Arnulfo Arias Madrid",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Unstable Democracy",
        situation: "You are Arnulfo Arias, three times president and twice overthrown, returned to power just weeks ago by the strangest coup in the Americas: police commander José Remón, the true kingmaker of Panama, had the election jury 'recount' the 1948 ballots and discover you had won all along. The doctor-caudillo's fiery nationalism still electrifies the crowds, but you rule at the pleasure of the man with the pistols, and Remón's patience has limits you have already tested twice before.\n\nThe Canal cuts your country in two — a ten-mile American strip of prosperity and power that pays Panama a pittance while Washington's warships glide through your waist. The people who cheered when the National Assembly rejected US bases in 1947 expect you to squeeze a better deal from the Zone. Your instincts pull toward the old dream: a new constitution, a stronger presidency, power without partners. Reach too far, and the police who made you can unmake you again.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Three-time president, twice deposed. The Canal watches.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("PER"),
        display_name: "Republic of Peru",
        leader_name: "Manuel A. Odría",
        leader_title: "President of the Military Junta",
        government: "Military Junta",
        situation: "Your tanks settled the argument in October 1948, when you rolled from Arequipa against the paralyzed government of Bustamante y Rivero and took Lima without a fight. Now General Manuel Odría rules by decree: APRA, the great populist party of the masses, is outlawed and hunted, and its leader Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre sits trapped in the Colombian embassy in Lima, an asylum standoff that embarrasses you before the world's lawyers and diplomats. The Communist Party is banned alongside. Order, you insist, comes first.\n\nThe economy rewards obedience. You have scrapped controls, opened the doors to foreign capital, and exporters of cotton, sugar, and minerals bless your name, while Washington approves of a firmly anti-communist Lima. This year you plan elections meant to transform the general into a legitimate president — with rules ensuring the outcome. But Arequipa, the city that made you, is restless, and stage-managed legitimacy has a way of demanding real blood.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The general's revolution needs a landslide.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("PHL"),
        display_name: "Republic of the Philippines",
        leader_name: "Elpidio Quirino",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Presidential Democracy",
        situation: "You are America's showcase in Asia, the first colony it freed, and the showcase is cracking. You won your own full term this November in an election so marred by fraud and violence that even your friends wince, and the legitimacy it cost you is ammunition for your enemies. Chief among them are the Huks, the wartime guerrillas turned Communist rebels who rule the night across Central Luzon, feeding on the grievances of tenant farmers your landlord-dominated Congress will not address. Your wife and children were killed in the war; you promised the survivors a republic worth the price.\n\nThe treasury bleeds dollars, forcing import controls, and Washington's patience is fraying even as its bases at Clark and Subic anchor you to the American line. With China fallen to Mao, your islands are suddenly the free world's eastern rampart. Reform the army, redeem the countryside, and outfight the Huks, or watch the showcase shatter.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Crush the Huk rebellion before it reaches Manila.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("POL"),
        display_name: "Republic of Poland",
        leader_name: "Bolesław Bierut",
        leader_title: "President and PZPR Chairman",
        government: "Communist One-Party State",
        situation: "You are Bolesław Bierut, Stalin's most trusted Pole, president of a nation the war nearly annihilated. One citizen in five is dead, Warsaw is being rebuilt street by street from a field of rubble, and the entire country has been carried westward — Wilno and Lwów lost to the Soviet Union, the Oder lands taken from Germany and filled with millions of uprooted settlers. Your United Workers' Party rules alone since the 1948 merger, and Gomułka, purged for his nationalist deviation, waits in disgrace as proof that no one is safe.\n\nNow Stalinization accelerates. A Soviet marshal, Rokossovsky, was installed as your defense minister in November, a humiliation Poles will not forget. The Six-Year Plan launches this year, promising steel mills and socialism while the countryside resists collectivization. The Catholic Church, unbroken, commands more hearts than the party ever will. You must satisfy Moscow, feed Poland, and hold down a nation that has never accepted foreign masters for long.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Rebuild a shattered nation under Stalin's gaze.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("POR"),
        display_name: "Portuguese Republic",
        leader_name: "António de Oliveira Salazar",
        leader_title: "President of the Council of Ministers",
        government: "Corporatist Dictatorship",
        situation: "You are the professor who became a dictator without ever raising his voice. For nearly two decades Salazar's Estado Novo has kept Portugal balanced, solvent, Catholic, and quiet — neutral through the World War, yet clever enough to lease the Azores to the Allies. That single card made you respectable: alone among Europe's authoritarian states, Portugal signed the North Atlantic Treaty as a founding member. The PIDE secret police handles those who mistake quiet for freedom; General Norton de Matos's opposition candidacy last year was permitted, then strangled.\n\nBeyond Lisbon stretches the true wager: an empire from Angola and Mozambique to Goa, Macau, and Timor, proclaimed not as colonies but as Portugal overseas, eternal and indivisible. The winds rising against empire in Asia and Africa have not yet reached your shores — but they will test whether a small, poor nation can hold a five-century inheritance. Keep the budget balanced, the alliance sweet, the empire silent, and history at bay.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "A quiet dictator, a five-century empire, one wager.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("PRC"),
        display_name: "People's Republic of China",
        leader_name: "Mao Zedong",
        leader_title: "Chairman of the Central People's Government",
        government: "Communist One-Party State",
        situation: "You have stood China up. Three months ago you proclaimed the People's Republic from the Tiananmen gate, ending a century of humiliation and more than twenty years of civil war. Your armies have swept the Nationalists from the mainland; Chiang Kai-shek clings to Taiwan and a few offshore islands. Now you sit in Moscow itself, enduring Stalin's calculated hospitality as you bargain for the treaty, credits, and industrial aid your shattered country desperately needs.\n\nAt home the tasks are immense. Inflation rages, the cities starve for capital, bandits and Nationalist stragglers roam the south, and Tibet remains beyond your writ. Land reform will remake the villages, but it will be neither gentle nor quiet. You must plan the conquest of Hainan and Taiwan while watching the Americans, whose intentions in Asia remain unreadable. The revolution has been won; now you must govern half a billion people and decide how much blood the new order requires.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "China has stood up. Now build it.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("PRK"),
        display_name: "Democratic People's Republic of Korea",
        leader_name: "Kim Il-sung",
        leader_title: "Premier",
        government: "Communist One-Party State",
        situation: "You are thirty-seven years old, a former guerrilla commander installed in Pyongyang, and you burn to finish what liberation began: a single Korea under the red banner. Your Democratic People's Republic, founded in 1948, commands the peninsula's industry and hydroelectric power, and Soviet advisers have built you a formidable army with tanks and aircraft the South utterly lacks. Battle-hardened Korean divisions are returning home from service in Mao's victorious armies.\n\nSkirmishes have bloodied the 38th parallel all year, and your guerrillas harry the southern mountains, though Rhee's police are grinding them down. Twice Stalin has refused your pleas to march south; you argue that the Americans, who withdrew their troops and hesitate to name Korea within their defense line, will not intervene. With China won and Moscow's bomb tested, you sense the moment turning. One bold stroke, you believe, could unify the fatherland within weeks. Such confidence will soon be tested.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "Reunify the fatherland, by any means necessary.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("PRY"),
        display_name: "Republic of Paraguay",
        leader_name: "Federico Chaves",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "One-Party Dominant Republic",
        situation: "Your country has burned through six presidents in two years. The 1947 civil war left the Colorado Party master of a shattered, half-emptied nation — hundreds of thousands fled into Argentina — and the victors promptly turned on each other: Moríñigo pushed out, then González, then Rolón, then Molas López, each toppled by the next Colorado faction. In September 1949 the party's warring wings finally settled on Federico Chaves, an elderly lawyer and democrático faction chief, as president. You inherit an exhausted treasury, an army swollen with coup-tested colonels, and a party that eats its own.\n\nParaguay is landlocked, poor, and squeezed between giants; Perón's Argentina holds your river trade in its fist and expects deference in return. Every opposition party is broken or exiled, so the only politics that matters happens inside the Colorado Party and the officer corps — the same combination that unmade your five predecessors. Consolidate power, revive the economy, and study your generals carefully. Some of them are studying you.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Six presidents in two years. Survive as the seventh.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ROC"),
        display_name: "Republic of China",
        leader_name: "Chiang Kai-shek",
        leader_title: "Director-General of the Kuomintang",
        government: "Nationalist One-Party State",
        situation: "Your republic has lost the mainland. Twenty-two years after the Northern Expedition made you master of China, you direct a rump government from Taipei, having overseen the retreat of soldiers, officials, and some two million refugees across the strait. Gold reserves, art treasures, and the remnants of your air force and navy came with you. Acting President Li Zongren has abandoned his post for New York; though you formally resigned a year ago, every thread of party, army, and state still runs through your hands.\n\nInvasion may come with the spring tides. Mao's armies mass along the Fujian coast, and Washington shows every sign of writing you off; the Americans speak of letting the dust settle. You must purge a defeated army into a loyal one, hold Hainan and the offshore islands if you can, and keep alive the claim that yours is the true government of all China. Survive this year, and history may yet turn in your favor.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "One island left. Retake the mainland.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ROU"),
        display_name: "Romanian People's Republic",
        leader_name: "Gheorghe Gheorghiu-Dej",
        leader_title: "General Secretary, Romanian Workers' Party",
        government: "Communist One-Party State",
        situation: "You are Gheorghe Gheorghiu-Dej, the railway electrician who survived Doftana prison and now leads the Romanian Workers' Party. King Michael was forced to abdicate at gunpoint in December 1947, and the People's Republic is barely two years old. But your power is not yet whole: you share it uneasily with the Muscovites — Ana Pauker at the Foreign Ministry and Vasile Luca at Finance — who spent the war in Moscow and enjoy their own lines to the Kremlin. In this system, only one faction ever survives.\n\nMeanwhile the Sovrom companies siphon Romania's oil and grain eastward as war booty dressed up as partnership. Collectivization, decreed last March, is igniting peasant resistance the Securitate answers with arrests. On the Dobruja steppe, political prisoners have begun digging the Danube–Black Sea Canal, a project measured in lives. Tito's heresy next door makes Moscow suspicious of every Balkan communist. Prove your loyalty — and outlast your rivals.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Outlast the Muscovites; only one faction survives.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("SAU"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Saudi Arabia",
        leader_name: "Abdulaziz Ibn Saud",
        leader_title: "King",
        government: "Absolute Monarchy",
        situation: "You are Abdulaziz Ibn Saud, the old lion who took Riyadh with forty men in 1902 and forged a kingdom from the warring tribes of Arabia. Now past seventy, you rule the peninsula as absolute monarch, custodian of Mecca and Medina, and father of dozens of princes. American oilmen at Dhahran pump ever more crude from the Eastern Province, and the royalties that trickled before the war now arrive in floods.\n\nThe money changes everything. Aramco wants new terms, and whispers of a fifty-fifty profit split like the one Venezuela won could multiply your treasury overnight. But wealth breeds appetites: your sons Saud and Faisal circle the succession, palaces rise faster than schools, and the British in the Gulf sheikhdoms contest your claims toward the Buraimi oases. You have kept the kingdom by balancing Wahhabi piety against modern machines and Washington against London. Your last years will decide whether your house inherits an empire or a quarrel.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Oil floods in. Spend it before your sons divide it.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("SLV"),
        display_name: "Republic of El Salvador",
        leader_name: "Óscar Osorio",
        leader_title: "Strongman of the Revolutionary Council",
        government: "Military Junta",
        situation: "You command the smallest and most crowded republic on the mainland, where a legendary fourteen families of coffee planters own the land and the memory of 1932 — when the army machine-gunned tens of thousands of rebellious peasants — still silences dissent. In December 1948 the young officers of the Majors' Coup overthrew the old general Castaneda Castro, promising a 'controlled revolution': modernization from above, without oligarchs and without communists. Major Óscar Osorio, the coup's dominant figure, has formally stepped off the ruling council to stand for president, but no one doubts whose hand steers the junta that remains.\n\nA new constitution is being drafted, elections are set for March, and Osorio's new party promises social security, public housing, and dams on the Lempa. The coffee barons watch warily; so do the exiled rivals across your borders. Reform enough to forestall another 1932, but never enough to threaten the barracks — that is the razor's edge you must walk.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "The majors have spoken; the oligarchy waits.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("SMR"),
        display_name: "Most Serene Republic of San Marino",
        leader_name: "Ermenegildo Gasperoni",
        leader_title: "General Secretary of the Sammarinese Communist Party",
        government: "Communist-Led Republic",
        situation: "The world's oldest republic is now its strangest Cold War outpost. Since 1945 San Marino has been governed by a freely elected communist-socialist coalition — re-elected just months ago — making this mountaintop of 12,000 citizens the only communist-led state in Western Europe. Two Captains Regent still swap office every six months as they have since the Middle Ages, but Ermenegildo Gasperoni's Communists set the course. And you are entirely surrounded by De Gasperi's Catholic, American-armed Italy.\n\nRome treats you as an intolerable curiosity, and the Vatican has excommunicated your voters wholesale. Your government dreams of funding its welfare schemes with a casino, a radio station and generous tax residency — every scheme certain to provoke Italian retaliation at the border. You have no army worth the name, no railway since the war, and no friends nearby; Moscow's warmth is distant and mostly rhetorical. Survival will take diplomacy, stubbornness, and sixteen centuries of practice at being small, free, and impossible to digest.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Western Europe's only communist state, surrounded and defiant.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("SOV"),
        display_name: "Union of Soviet Socialist Republics",
        leader_name: "Joseph Stalin",
        leader_title: "General Secretary",
        government: "Communist One-Party State",
        situation: "You rule the Soviet Union with absolute authority, fresh from seventieth birthday celebrations that filled Red Square with adulation. The past year brought triumphs: your scientists detonated an atomic bomb in August, shattering the American monopoly, and Mao's communists conquered China, vastly enlarging the socialist world. Mao himself now waits in Moscow, negotiating an alliance treaty. In Germany your new eastern republic stands as a bulwark, though the Berlin blockade ended in frustration.\n\nYet vigilance is eternal. Tito's Yugoslavia has defied you and survived. In Leningrad, party officials grew too ambitious, and the purges have begun again. Your armies are unmatched in Europe, but American bombers ring your frontiers. Now Kim Il-sung petitions insistently for permission to reunify Korea by force. Grant it, and you may hand socialism a cheap victory, or draw the Americans deep into Asia. The correlation of forces, you judge, has shifted. Perhaps it is time to probe.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "The bomb is yours. Now win the world.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("SWE"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Sweden",
        leader_name: "Tage Erlander",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Constitutional Monarchy",
        situation: "You govern Sweden, untouched by war for a hundred and thirty-five years. While Europe burned twice, your factories, cities and gold reserves survived intact, and now your industry hums at full capacity supplying a continent rebuilding itself. Your effort to bind Norway and Denmark into a neutral Scandinavian defense union collapsed last year; both chose the Atlantic Pact instead. Sweden stands alone — by choice — armed to the teeth, with one of Europe's strongest air forces and an arms industry that builds its own jets.\n\nNeutrality is a policy, not a sentiment. Your Social Democratic government quietly cooperates with the West while trading with the East, and Moscow watches with suspicion. The Baltic before you is filling with spies, and the fate of Raoul Wallenberg, vanished into Soviet custody, remains an open wound. At home you are building a welfare state the world will study. Abroad, you must be strong enough that no one tests your independence.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Armed to the teeth, allied to no one.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("SYR"),
        display_name: "Syrian Republic",
        leader_name: "Adib al-Shishakli",
        leader_title: "Army Strongman",
        government: "Military-Dominated Republic",
        situation: "You are Colonel Adib al-Shishakli, and twelve days ago you carried out Syria's third coup in a single year. Husni al-Za'im seized power in March and was shot in August by Sami al-Hinnawi, whom you have just arrested for scheming to merge Syria into Hashemite Iraq. You have learned the coup's lesson better than its victims: rule from the shadows. Venerable Hashim al-Atassi keeps the presidency, Khalid al-Azm forms a cabinet, and a Constituent Assembly drafts a constitution — while every minister knows the army's tanks decide what stands.\n\nThe republic beneath you is raw: humiliated in Palestine, locked in border quarrels with Israel over the demilitarized zones, and courted by rival Iraqi and Egyptian blocs competing for Arab leadership. Politicians in Damascus dream of restoring true civilian rule; Baathists, communists, and Saadeh's nationalists recruit in your officer corps as eagerly as you once did. You hold the strings today. In Syria, that guarantees nothing tomorrow.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Three coups in one year. The fourth is yours to prevent.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("THA"),
        display_name: "Kingdom of Thailand",
        leader_name: "Plaek Phibunsongkhram",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Military Dictatorship",
        situation: "You are Field Marshal Phibun, the wartime dictator who allied with Japan, was disgraced, and then returned to power on the shoulders of the army's 1947 coup. The Cold War has been your rehabilitation: to Washington, an anti-Communist strongman in Bangkok suddenly looks less like a collaborator and more like an asset. You have already crushed the Palace Rebellion of your old rival Pridi, whose partisans still plot from exile, and the navy's loyalty remains an open question. The young King Bhumibol studies in Switzerland, his brother's death in 1946 still an unhealed wound around the throne.\n\nThailand alone in Southeast Asia was never colonized, surviving by bending like bamboo in the wind. Now Mao rules China, the Viet Minh burns through Indochina next door, and millions of overseas Chinese live in your cities. Rice exports fill the treasury; American aid could fill the arsenal. Choose your patron shrewdly, and keep your generals closer than your enemies.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Bamboo bends with the wind and never breaks.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("TUR"),
        display_name: "Republic of Turkey",
        leader_name: "İsmet İnönü",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Parliamentary Republic",
        situation: "You are İsmet İnönü, Atatürk's chief lieutenant and his successor as President since 1938. You steered Turkey through the World War without firing a shot, only to face Stalin's demands for Kars, Ardahan, and bases on the Straits. You answered by turning West: the Truman Doctrine sends dollars and arms, Marshall aid rebuilds your roads, and Ankara knocks insistently on the door of the new Atlantic alliance.\n\nAt home you have gambled everything on an experiment no one-party regime has survived: free elections. Since 1946 the Democrat Party of Celal Bayar and Adnan Menderes has grown from tolerated opposition into a movement filling the squares of Anatolia, promising farmers tractors and believers an easing of strict secularism. A new election law guarantees secret ballots and judicial oversight, and the vote comes in May. If you win, you vindicate your republic; if you lose, you must decide whether Atatürk's heirs surrender power peacefully. The world is watching what Turkish democracy is worth.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Face the free election that will define Turkish democracy.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("URY"),
        display_name: "Oriental Republic of Uruguay",
        leader_name: "Luis Batlle Berres",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Liberal Democracy",
        situation: "You govern the Switzerland of America. While its neighbors trade coups and caudillos, Uruguay enjoys free elections, a free press, the eight-hour day, pensions, and public schools — the welfare state built by the great José Batlle y Ordóñez and now stewarded by his nephew, President Luis Batlle Berres. Wool and beef exports feed hungry postwar markets, Montevideo's cafés hum with émigrés and ideas, and your football squad prepares to sail for Brazil and the World Cup, where nobody gives the little country a chance.\n\nProsperity has its termites. The whole edifice rests on selling meat and wool to the world at good prices, and the state payroll grows faster than the pastures that pay for it. Within your own Colorado Party, the heirs of Batlle quarrel over replacing the presidency itself with a Swiss-style collegiate executive, and the conservative Herreristas wait for batllismo to stumble. Keep the export boom alive and the model endures; let it slip, and even model democracies discover their limits.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Defend the hemisphere's model democracy.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("USA"),
        display_name: "United States of America",
        leader_name: "Harry S. Truman",
        leader_title: "President",
        government: "Presidential Democracy",
        situation: "You govern the wealthiest and most powerful nation on Earth, yet 1950 opens under a shadow. In August the Soviets detonated an atomic bomb years ahead of schedule, ending your nuclear monopoly. In October, China fell to Mao's communists, and Washington echoes with the bitter question of who lost it. The Marshall Plan is rebuilding Western Europe and the new NATO alliance binds it to you, but containment is being tested on every front.\n\nAt home, President Truman presses his Fair Deal against a skeptical Congress while spy trials feed a growing hunt for subversives, and ambitious senators sense opportunity in the fear. Your advisers debate a terrible new weapon, the hydrogen bomb, and a sweeping rearmament of the free world. Asia demands answers: should you defend Formosa? What of Korea, where your last combat troops departed only months ago? The decisions of this year will define the shape of the Cold War.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "Lead the free world into the atomic age.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("VAT"),
        display_name: "Vatican City State",
        leader_name: "Pius XII",
        leader_title: "Pope",
        government: "Theocratic Elective Monarchy",
        situation: "At midnight on Christmas Eve, Pius XII opened the Holy Door: 1950 is a Holy Year, and millions of pilgrims are converging on Rome to see the Church proclaim itself the bulwark against godless materialism. Six months ago the Holy Office decreed that Catholics who profess communism excommunicate themselves — a spiritual declaration of war on the Kremlin. Behind the Iron Curtain that war is already real: Cardinal Mindszenty sits in a Hungarian prison, and bishops from Warsaw to Beijing face show trials.\n\nYou rule the smallest state on earth — a hundred-odd acres — yet command the allegiance of four hundred million souls, and Italy's 1948 election proved what that allegiance can do at a ballot box. This year holds great cards: the excavations beneath St. Peter's may reveal the Apostle's own tomb, and the dogma of the Assumption awaits solemn definition. Stalin once sneered at how many divisions you command. Show him what faith without divisions can move.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Wield the keys of heaven against the Kremlin.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("VEN"),
        display_name: "United States of Venezuela",
        leader_name: "Carlos Delgado Chalbaud",
        leader_title: "President of the Military Junta",
        government: "Military Junta",
        situation: "You preside over the hemisphere's great oil spigot — and over a democracy you helped strangle. In November 1948 the army overthrew novelist-president Rómulo Gallegos, ending the brief reformist trienio of Acción Democrática. Now a three-man junta rules from Caracas: you, the cultured, Paris-educated Carlos Delgado Chalbaud, flanked by the ambitious Marcos Pérez Jiménez, whose appetite for power is no secret. Acción Democrática is banned and driven underground, Rómulo Betancourt plots from exile, and the oilfield unions simmer under military watch.\n\nPetroleum is your blessing and your leash. The fifty-fifty profit split wrung from the foreign companies fills the treasury, and Washington values barrels over ballots, so the dollars flow either way. You have promised eventual elections that neither your colleagues nor the companies are eager to see. Steer between the exiles who want your head and the general at your shoulder who wants your chair — in Caracas, the knife can come from any direction.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Rule the oil junta — watch your back.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("YEM"),
        display_name: "Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen",
        leader_name: "Ahmad bin Yahya",
        leader_title: "Imam and King",
        government: "Theocratic Absolute Monarchy",
        situation: "You are Imam Ahmad bin Yahya, king and religious leader of a mountain realm that has barely admitted the twentieth century. Two years ago conspirators murdered your father, Imam Yahya, in the Alwaziri coup; you rallied the northern tribes, retook Sanaa and let them sack it as payment, and took your revenge with the sword. Now you rule from Taiz, suspicious of everyone, guarding the old ways: hostages to bind the tribes, foreign visitors counted on one hand, hardly a paved road in the kingdom.\n\nYet the world presses in. The British in Aden dispute your southern frontier and court the protectorate sheikhs you consider stolen subjects. Foreign radios preach Arab awakening to your young officers, some of whom studied abroad and came home dreaming dangerous dreams. You must decide how much modernity to admit — a few engineers, a few arms deals, perhaps even overtures to distant powers — without cracking open the sealed kingdom that keeps your dynasty alive.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Keep the medieval kingdom sealed against the twentieth century.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("YUG"),
        display_name: "Federal People's Republic of Yugoslavia",
        leader_name: "Josip Broz Tito",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister and Marshal of Yugoslavia",
        government: "Communist One-Party State",
        situation: "You are Tito, the only man ever to defy Stalin and live. Expelled from the Cominform in June 1948 as a traitor to socialism, you have refused to kneel. Now the entire Soviet bloc strangles you with economic blockade, border incidents crackle nightly along frontiers with Hungary, Romania, Bulgaria and Albania, and your army watches for the invasion Moscow's propaganda promises daily. At home you purge suspected Cominformists ruthlessly — the prison island of Goli Otok is filling — for a state besieged cannot afford divided loyalty.\n\nYet you are not without cards. In October 1949 you won a UN Security Council seat over the Kremlin's public fury, and quiet American credits have begun to flow to the heretic. The Greek border you closed last summer bought a measure of calm in the south. But drought stalks the countryside and the blockade bites deep. Between the blocs, you must invent something the world has never seen — or be crushed.",
        interesting: true,
        hook: "Defy Stalin and live.",
    ),
    NationMeta(
        tag: ("ZAF"),
        display_name: "Union of South Africa",
        leader_name: "Daniel François Malan",
        leader_title: "Prime Minister",
        government: "Parliamentary Apartheid State",
        situation: "Eighteen months ago your National Party stunned the world, unseating the war hero Smuts on a single promise: apartheid. The first bricks are laid — mixed marriages are banned — and your drafters are preparing the great statutes of 1950: population registration, group areas, and a sweeping bill to suppress communism that will define opposition itself as subversion. Afrikanerdom is ascendant, the gold mines are booming, and the dream of a republic free of Britain stirs in your caucus.\n\nBut resistance is organizing. The African National Congress has just adopted a militant Programme of Action, pushed through by young men named Mandela, Sisulu and Tambo. India attacks you at the United Nations; your refusal to surrender South-West Africa draws international censure. Yet the Cold War is your shield — the West needs your gold, your uranium and the Cape sea route. You hold the machinery of a modern state and a design without precedent. History will judge what you build with them.",
        interesting: false,
        hook: "Gold, uranium, and a design the world will not forgive.",
    ),
]
